[2026-11-18T12:23:34.355Z] [DEBUG] [client] MCP client request body, backend=safeoutputs, body={"method":"tools/call","params":{"name":"create_issue","arguments":{"title":"Chery E5 (renamed Omoda E5): current battery and DC figures disagree","body":"The **Chery E5** is the renamed Omoda E5, which is in the file as `omoda-e5-2024` (61.1 kWh, 430 km, 80 kW DC est.). The Green Vehicle Guide homepage now lists it under that name (\"Chery E5\" in Top CO2 performing vehicles), and The Beep reports a single MY26 Ultimate variant at $37,990 drive-away.\n\nSources disagree about the current car's figures, and Chery's Australian site couldn't be read (cheryaustralia.com.au presents a certificate for a different domain), so I left the file unchanged:\n\n| Figure | File | Reported for MY26 |\n|---|---|---|\n| Battery | 61.1 kWh (58 usable est.) | 58.9 kWh (carsales research page, The Beep, EV-wiki) |\n| Peak DC | 80 kW (est.) | 130 kW (EV-wiki, The Beep article: \"30-80 per cent in around 30 minutes at up to 130kW\"), or 155 kW (carsales, listed against \"DC Charging Time (at Max kW)\") |\n| WLTP | 430 km | 430 km (all sources agree) |\n\n**To decide:**\n1. Should the MY26 car get its own entry (e.g. `chery-e5-2026`, make Chery), with `omoda-e5-2024` given `untilYear`? The rename happened in 2025.\n2. Which peak DC figure is right for the Australian MY26 car, 130 kW or 155 kW? Chery's spec sheet or brochure would settle it.\n\nUntil then the existing 80 kW figure is the cautious one for planning: it may make charging stops look longer than they are, but never shorter."},"_meta":{"claudecode/toolUseId":"toolu_013mbcExU9Uq7ri1Mykixaie","progressToken":4}},"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":4}
